WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.414
The A to Z of isms... populism.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.730
Populism sounds like democracy in action.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.690
It's all about looking after the little guy,
hearing what ordinary people want

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.980
and giving it to them -or at least promising to give it to them.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:22.109
Oddly though, 'populist' is almost always a pejorative term -no one really wants the label.

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.820
Most populist leaders would rather
see themselves as inspiring grassroot movements.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.189
Populism has been around at
least since ancient Roman times,

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:36.930
with emperor's lavishing money on food and entertainment, bread and circuses to appeal

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:42.149
to the masses.  The English Civil War, the
French Revolution, the Brexit referendum,

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.890
Donald Trump's rise to power -all these
came from populist groundswells of

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.390
opinion against elites and the status
quo. When a populist movement gets big

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.379
enough and doesn't get what it wants, it
gives the mainstream the finger by

00:00:55.379 --> 00:01:00.149
winning an election. So what could be
wrong with a charismatic leader focusing

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.080
the will of the people and millions
voting for what they believe in?

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.960
When populists choose to vote rather than revolt

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.820
isn't this just what democracy looks like when it's working? Well, some people see it

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:16.380
that way... But there's more to democracy
than voting, at least more to liberal

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.869
democracy. In functioning democracies
disagreement and dissent are part of the

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:26.820
political process. Political debate
involves reason, evidence, challenge and

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:32.070
argument. Free speech and a free press
are vital to this.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:36.270
Populists, in contrast, love stirring hearts with rhetoric, aren't renowned for using

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.909
evidence and argument, and are keen on
dividing the world into 'us' and 'them'.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:45.869
They're prone to scapegoating
journalists, elites and foreigners and to

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:50.700
conspiracy theories about why the world
isn't as they want it to be.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.409
Political opponents are dismissed as not
understanding what ordinary people want.

00:01:54.409 --> 00:01:59.729
The real danger in populism though is
that it can morph into authoritarianism.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:03.780
When populist leaders believe they're in
touch with the spirit of the people

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.630
heaven help anyone who stands in
their way.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:11.039
The fervor of populist politics could be
truly terrifying, as the French Revolution demonstrated.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.270
The philosopherJean-Jacques Rousseau, whose ideas contributed to it,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:21.000
provides a chilling warning of where unfettered populism can end up.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.000
He declared that citizens who
disagreed with the general will of the people,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.000
should be forced to be free. That's a very long way from sticking up for the little guy.

